BATCH.english = [
  {
    id: 'en_w1_01', type: 'text', difficulty: 'basic',
    topicId: 'word_formation', topicZh: 'Word Formation', topicEn: 'Word Formation',
    question: 'Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in brackets. Write the word only.\n1. The committee reached a decision after a long ______ (DISCUSS).\n2. Her ______ (ABLE) to stay calm under pressure impressed the interviewers.\n3. The instructions were so ______ (CLEAR) that nobody knew what to do. (Use a negative prefix.)\n4. He spoke ______ (CONFIDENT) even though he had not prepared.\n5. The scheme was widely criticised as ______ (PRACTICAL). (Use a negative prefix.)',
    questionEn: 'Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in brackets. Write the word only.\n1. The committee reached a decision after a long ______ (DISCUSS).\n2. Her ______ (ABLE) to stay calm under pressure impressed the interviewers.\n3. The instructions were so ______ (CLEAR) that nobody knew what to do. (Use a negative prefix.)\n4. He spoke ______ (CONFIDENT) even though he had not prepared.\n5. The scheme was widely criticised as ______ (PRACTICAL). (Use a negative prefix.)',
    referenceAnswer: '1. discussion (noun after "a long")\n2. ability (noun as subject of the sentence; note the spelling change from ABLE)\n3. unclear (adjective with a negative prefix; "so ______ that" needs an adjective)\n4. confidently (adverb modifying the verb "spoke")\n5. impractical (adjective; the prefix for PRACTICAL is im-, not un-)\n\nEach answer is fixed by two things: the part of speech the slot requires, and the meaning the sentence needs. Decide the part of speech first from the words around the gap, then choose the affix.',
    referenceAnswerEn: '1. discussion (noun after "a long")\n2. ability (noun as subject of the sentence; note the spelling change from ABLE)\n3. unclear (adjective with a negative prefix; "so ______ that" needs an adjective)\n4. confidently (adverb modifying the verb "spoke")\n5. impractical (adjective; the prefix for PRACTICAL is im-, not un-)\n\nEach answer is fixed by two things: the part of speech the slot requires, and the meaning the sentence needs. Decide the part of speech first from the words around the gap, then choose the affix.',
    explanation: 'Word-formation items are decided by grammar before vocabulary. Read what sits immediately before and after the gap — an article or adjective points to a noun, a verb points to an adverb, "so … that" points to an adjective. Only then ask which affix carries the meaning. Two traps appear here: ABLE to ability involves a spelling change that a mechanical -ity ending would miss, and PRACTICAL takes im-, not un-, because English negative prefixes are not interchangeable. Writing a correctly spelt word of the wrong part of speech scores nothing, which is why the grammatical decision comes first.',
    explanationEn: 'Word-formation items are decided by grammar before vocabulary. Read what sits immediately before and after the gap — an article or adjective points to a noun, a verb points to an adverb, "so … that" points to an adjective. Only then ask which affix carries the meaning. Two traps appear here: ABLE to ability involves a spelling change that a mechanical -ity ending would miss, and PRACTICAL takes im-, not un-, because English negative prefixes are not interchangeable. Writing a correctly spelt word of the wrong part of speech scores nothing, which is why the grammatical decision comes first.',
  },
  {
    id: 'en_w1_02', type: 'long', difficulty: 'intermediate', marks: 8, suggestedMinutes: 14,
    topicId: 'p1_inference', topicZh: 'Reading · Inference & Implication', topicEn: 'Reading · Inference & Implication',
    question: 'Read the passage, then answer the questions.\n\n"The school announced the new timetable on a Friday afternoon, three days before it took effect. A notice appeared on the board outside the staff room; no email was sent. When a group of students asked whether they could discuss it, they were told the consultation period had closed. Several teachers said afterwards that they had learnt of the change at the same time as the students."\n\n(a) What can be inferred about how the decision was made? Support your answer with two details from the passage.\n(b) The writer mentions that the notice appeared "on a Friday afternoon, three days before it took effect". What is the effect of including this detail?\n(c) Identify the writer\'s attitude towards the school. Quote one phrase that reveals it and explain how it does so.',
    questionEn: 'Read the passage, then answer the questions.\n\n"The school announced the new timetable on a Friday afternoon, three days before it took effect. A notice appeared on the board outside the staff room; no email was sent. When a group of students asked whether they could discuss it, they were told the consultation period had closed. Several teachers said afterwards that they had learnt of the change at the same time as the students."\n\n(a) What can be inferred about how the decision was made? Support your answer with two details from the passage.\n(b) The writer mentions that the notice appeared "on a Friday afternoon, three days before it took effect". What is the effect of including this detail?\n(c) Identify the writer\'s attitude towards the school. Quote one phrase that reveals it and explain how it does so.',
    referenceAnswer: '(a) It can be inferred that the decision was made by a small group without consulting those affected. Two supporting details: the teachers "learnt of the change at the same time as the students", which shows staff were not involved in making it; and students were told "the consultation period had closed", although no student appears to have been aware that one was open.\n(b) The timing detail makes the announcement look designed to limit response. A Friday afternoon is the point in the week when the fewest people are present, and three days leaves no working time to object before the change begins. Stating the two facts together lets the reader draw that conclusion without the writer asserting it.\n(c) The attitude is critical, though expressed indirectly. The phrase "no email was sent" reveals it: the writer records the absence of the obvious means of reaching everyone, and a plain statement of what was not done invites the reader to judge it. Nothing in the sentence is openly hostile, which is what makes the criticism effective.',
    referenceAnswerEn: '(a) It can be inferred that the decision was made by a small group without consulting those affected. Two supporting details: the teachers "learnt of the change at the same time as the students", which shows staff were not involved in making it; and students were told "the consultation period had closed", although no student appears to have been aware that one was open.\n(b) The timing detail makes the announcement look designed to limit response. A Friday afternoon is the point in the week when the fewest people are present, and three days leaves no working time to object before the change begins. Stating the two facts together lets the reader draw that conclusion without the writer asserting it.\n(c) The attitude is critical, though expressed indirectly. The phrase "no email was sent" reveals it: the writer records the absence of the obvious means of reaching everyone, and a plain statement of what was not done invites the reader to judge it. Nothing in the sentence is openly hostile, which is what makes the criticism effective.',
    explanation: 'Inference questions ask what the passage makes it reasonable to conclude, not what it states. The commonest error is answering (a) by copying a sentence out; a quotation is evidence for an inference, never the inference itself. Part (b) tests why a detail is there at all — every specific in a short passage is chosen, and the answer should say what work the choice does. Part (c) is the hardest, because the passage contains no adjective of judgement: the attitude is carried by selection, by which facts the writer chose to place side by side. Answering "the writer is angry" overstates it; the register is restrained, and saying so is part of a full answer.',
    explanationEn: 'Inference questions ask what the passage makes it reasonable to conclude, not what it states. The commonest error is answering (a) by copying a sentence out; a quotation is evidence for an inference, never the inference itself. Part (b) tests why a detail is there at all — every specific in a short passage is chosen, and the answer should say what work the choice does. Part (c) is the hardest, because the passage contains no adjective of judgement: the attitude is carried by selection, by which facts the writer chose to place side by side. Answering "the writer is angry" overstates it; the register is restrained, and saying so is part of a full answer.',
    markingScheme: '(a) 3 marks — a defensible inference about how the decision was made (1); two supporting details drawn from the passage (1 each). A quotation offered without an inference earns nothing.\n(b) 2 marks — identifying the effect as limiting the chance to respond (1); linking it to both the day of the week and the three-day gap (1).\n(c) 3 marks — the attitude named as critical or disapproving (1); one relevant phrase quoted (1); an explanation of how that phrase carries the attitude (1). Naming an attitude with no textual evidence earns nothing.',
    markingSchemeEn: '(a) 3 marks — a defensible inference about how the decision was made (1); two supporting details drawn from the passage (1 each). A quotation offered without an inference earns nothing.\n(b) 2 marks — identifying the effect as limiting the chance to respond (1); linking it to both the day of the week and the three-day gap (1).\n(c) 3 marks — the attitude named as critical or disapproving (1); one relevant phrase quoted (1); an explanation of how that phrase carries the attitude (1). Naming an attitude with no textual evidence earns nothing.',
  },
  {
    id: 'en_w1_03', type: 'long', difficulty: 'intermediate', marks: 10, suggestedMinutes: 30,
    topicId: 'genre_tone', topicZh: 'Genre, Tone & Register', topicEn: 'Genre, Tone & Register',
    question: 'Your school library has decided to close two hours earlier on weekdays because of staffing costs. Many students use it after lessons.\n\nWrite a letter of about 200 words to the school principal, asking for the decision to be reconsidered. You should:\n- state clearly what you are writing about and why;\n- give two reasons, each supported with something concrete;\n- propose one workable alternative;\n- close appropriately.\n\nSign your letter "Chris Wong".',
    questionEn: 'Your school library has decided to close two hours earlier on weekdays because of staffing costs. Many students use it after lessons.\n\nWrite a letter of about 200 words to the school principal, asking for the decision to be reconsidered. You should:\n- state clearly what you are writing about and why;\n- give two reasons, each supported with something concrete;\n- propose one workable alternative;\n- close appropriately.\n\nSign your letter "Chris Wong".',
    referenceAnswer: 'This task is self-assessed. Rather than a model letter to copy, here is what a strong response contains — compare your own work against each point.\n\nFormat: a salutation to a named or titled reader ("Dear Principal"), a clear opening line naming the subject, paragraphs that each do one job, and a sign-off that matches the salutation ("Yours sincerely" with a named reader).\n\nRegister: formal but not stiff. A letter to a principal asking for a reversal should be respectful and specific. Contractions, exclamation marks and rhetorical questions ("Don\'t you think this is unfair?") push the register too low. Legalistic or over-elaborate vocabulary pushes it too high and reads as insincere.\n\nContent: two reasons, each anchored to something concrete — for example, students who travel far and cannot study at home, or the period before examinations when the after-school hours matter most. A general claim that "students need the library" is not yet a reason.\n\nThe alternative: one proposal that acknowledges the stated constraint. Since the reason given was staffing cost, a workable alternative addresses cost — a student volunteer rota supervised by one member of staff, or later opening only in the weeks before examinations. A proposal that ignores the constraint ("just hire more staff") does not answer the situation.\n\nClose: a specific request, such as asking for the matter to be raised at the next meeting.\n\nCommon weaknesses: writing an essay about the value of reading instead of a letter; giving three or four thin reasons instead of two developed ones; and proposing an alternative that costs more than the decision was meant to save.',
    referenceAnswerEn: 'This task is self-assessed. Rather than a model letter to copy, here is what a strong response contains — compare your own work against each point.\n\nFormat: a salutation to a named or titled reader ("Dear Principal"), a clear opening line naming the subject, paragraphs that each do one job, and a sign-off that matches the salutation ("Yours sincerely" with a named reader).\n\nRegister: formal but not stiff. A letter to a principal asking for a reversal should be respectful and specific. Contractions, exclamation marks and rhetorical questions ("Don\'t you think this is unfair?") push the register too low. Legalistic or over-elaborate vocabulary pushes it too high and reads as insincere.\n\nContent: two reasons, each anchored to something concrete — for example, students who travel far and cannot study at home, or the period before examinations when the after-school hours matter most. A general claim that "students need the library" is not yet a reason.\n\nThe alternative: one proposal that acknowledges the stated constraint. Since the reason given was staffing cost, a workable alternative addresses cost — a student volunteer rota supervised by one member of staff, or later opening only in the weeks before examinations. A proposal that ignores the constraint ("just hire more staff") does not answer the situation.\n\nClose: a specific request, such as asking for the matter to be raised at the next meeting.\n\nCommon weaknesses: writing an essay about the value of reading instead of a letter; giving three or four thin reasons instead of two developed ones; and proposing an alternative that costs more than the decision was meant to save.',
    explanation: 'Register is the part candidates most often misjudge, and it is judged against the reader, not against a fixed idea of "formal English". A principal is a senior reader who can reverse the decision, so the letter needs to be courteous and to give reasons that person could act on. The second thing worth attention is the alternative: the question supplies the constraint (staffing costs) precisely so that a proposal can be tested against it. Answering as though the constraint had not been stated is what separates a competent letter from a strong one. Length matters too — at 200 words there is room for two developed reasons, not four listed ones.',
    explanationEn: 'Register is the part candidates most often misjudge, and it is judged against the reader, not against a fixed idea of "formal English". A principal is a senior reader who can reverse the decision, so the letter needs to be courteous and to give reasons that person could act on. The second thing worth attention is the alternative: the question supplies the constraint (staffing costs) precisely so that a proposal can be tested against it. Answering as though the constraint had not been stated is what separates a competent letter from a strong one. Length matters too — at 200 words there is room for two developed reasons, not four listed ones.',
    markingScheme: 'Compare your own letter against four areas. Nothing here is machine-marked; you decide where your work stands.\n\nFormat and conventions — salutation, opening statement of purpose, paragraphing, matching sign-off, correct signature.\nRegister and tone — consistently formal and respectful, no contractions or exclamations, no over-formal padding.\nContent — two reasons, each supported by something concrete rather than asserted; one alternative that responds to the stated cost constraint.\nLanguage — sentence variety, accurate tenses and articles, vocabulary suited to a formal letter.\n\nA fair way to self-assess: read your letter as the principal. Would you know within the first two lines what is being asked? Is there a proposal you could act on tomorrow?',
    markingSchemeEn: 'Compare your own letter against four areas. Nothing here is machine-marked; you decide where your work stands.\n\nFormat and conventions — salutation, opening statement of purpose, paragraphing, matching sign-off, correct signature.\nRegister and tone — consistently formal and respectful, no contractions or exclamations, no over-formal padding.\nContent — two reasons, each supported by something concrete rather than asserted; one alternative that responds to the stated cost constraint.\nLanguage — sentence variety, accurate tenses and articles, vocabulary suited to a formal letter.\n\nA fair way to self-assess: read your letter as the principal. Would you know within the first two lines what is being asked? Is there a proposal you could act on tomorrow?',
  },
  {
    id: 'en_w1_04', type: 'text', difficulty: 'intermediate',
    topicId: 'cloze', topicZh: 'Cloze & Usage', topicEn: 'Cloze & Usage',
    question: 'Fill each blank with ONE word only.\n\n"Although the trial was small, the results were consistent (1) ______ earlier findings. The researchers were careful (2) ______ to overstate what they had shown, pointing out that the sample (3) ______ drawn entirely from one district. They now intend to repeat the study (4) ______ a larger group, and have made their data available so (5) ______ others can check the analysis."',
    questionEn: 'Fill each blank with ONE word only.\n\n"Although the trial was small, the results were consistent (1) ______ earlier findings. The researchers were careful (2) ______ to overstate what they had shown, pointing out that the sample (3) ______ drawn entirely from one district. They now intend to repeat the study (4) ______ a larger group, and have made their data available so (5) ______ others can check the analysis."',
    referenceAnswer: '(1) with — the fixed collocation is "consistent with", not "consistent to".\n(2) not — "careful not to overstate"; the sentence needs a negative before the infinitive.\n(3) was — the passive "was drawn", agreeing with the singular "sample".\n(4) with — "repeat the study with a larger group"; "on" is also defensible.\n(5) that — "so that" introduces a clause of purpose.\n\nEach blank is decided by structure rather than by meaning alone: a fixed collocation (1), a negative infinitive (2), verb form and agreement (3), a preposition (4) and a two-word conjunction (5).',
    referenceAnswerEn: '(1) with — the fixed collocation is "consistent with", not "consistent to".\n(2) not — "careful not to overstate"; the sentence needs a negative before the infinitive.\n(3) was — the passive "was drawn", agreeing with the singular "sample".\n(4) with — "repeat the study with a larger group"; "on" is also defensible.\n(5) that — "so that" introduces a clause of purpose.\n\nEach blank is decided by structure rather than by meaning alone: a fixed collocation (1), a negative infinitive (2), verb form and agreement (3), a preposition (4) and a two-word conjunction (5).',
    explanation: 'Open cloze rewards reading the whole sentence before writing anything. Blank (2) is the one most often missed: the sentence says the researchers avoided overstating, so a negative is required, yet the gap sits in a position where a positive reading looks grammatical. Blank (3) tests agreement across a distance — the subject is "the sample", singular, even though "findings" and "researchers" are the nearest plurals. Blank (5) is a reminder that some conjunctions are two words and the second half can be tested on its own. Where more than one word fits, as in (4), examiners accept any answer that is both grammatical and consistent with the meaning.',
    explanationEn: 'Open cloze rewards reading the whole sentence before writing anything. Blank (2) is the one most often missed: the sentence says the researchers avoided overstating, so a negative is required, yet the gap sits in a position where a positive reading looks grammatical. Blank (3) tests agreement across a distance — the subject is "the sample", singular, even though "findings" and "researchers" are the nearest plurals. Blank (5) is a reminder that some conjunctions are two words and the second half can be tested on its own. Where more than one word fits, as in (4), examiners accept any answer that is both grammatical and consistent with the meaning.',
  },
  {
    id: 'en_w1_05', type: 'long', difficulty: 'hard', marks: 12, suggestedMinutes: 35,
    topicId: 'integrated', topicZh: 'Integrated Skills', topicEn: 'Integrated Skills',
    question: 'DATA FILE\n\nSource A — Extract from a district survey (150 respondents aged 12–18)\n- 68% say they would use a late-night study space if one existed\n- 41% say the main obstacle to studying at home is lack of quiet space\n- 22% report travelling more than 40 minutes to reach the nearest public library\n\nSource B — Comment from a district councillor\n"We have space in the community hall on three evenings a week. What we do not have is supervision. Any proposal has to answer that before it answers anything else."\n\nSource C — Note from a school social worker\n"Late opening helps the students who need it most, but it also carries risk. Young people going home after ten at night need a plan for getting there safely, and someone has to be responsible for knowing who is still in the building."\n\nTASK\nYou are the chairperson of a student union. Write a proposal of about 250 words to the district council, arguing for a supervised evening study space in the community hall.\n\nYou must:\n- use at least two figures from Source A, and say what each one shows;\n- respond directly to the constraint raised in Source B;\n- address at least one of the concerns in Source C;\n- write in a register suitable for a council submission.',
    questionEn: 'DATA FILE\n\nSource A — Extract from a district survey (150 respondents aged 12–18)\n- 68% say they would use a late-night study space if one existed\n- 41% say the main obstacle to studying at home is lack of quiet space\n- 22% report travelling more than 40 minutes to reach the nearest public library\n\nSource B — Comment from a district councillor\n"We have space in the community hall on three evenings a week. What we do not have is supervision. Any proposal has to answer that before it answers anything else."\n\nSource C — Note from a school social worker\n"Late opening helps the students who need it most, but it also carries risk. Young people going home after ten at night need a plan for getting there safely, and someone has to be responsible for knowing who is still in the building."\n\nTASK\nYou are the chairperson of a student union. Write a proposal of about 250 words to the district council, arguing for a supervised evening study space in the community hall.\n\nYou must:\n- use at least two figures from Source A, and say what each one shows;\n- respond directly to the constraint raised in Source B;\n- address at least one of the concerns in Source C;\n- write in a register suitable for a council submission.',
    referenceAnswer: 'This task is self-assessed. What follows is what a strong proposal does, point by point.\n\nUsing the data: a figure must be interpreted, not merely quoted. "68% would use the space" states demand; "41% cite lack of quiet space at home" explains why the demand exists and shows the need is about conditions rather than preference; "22% travel more than 40 minutes" establishes that the existing provision does not reach this group. A proposal that lists all three percentages without saying what each shows has copied rather than used the data.\n\nAnswering Source B: the councillor names supervision as the first obstacle, so the proposal must put supervision early and make it concrete — who supervises, on what rota, at what cost, and who is accountable. A vague promise that "supervision can be arranged" does not meet the constraint the source sets out.\n\nAnswering Source C: the social worker raises two distinct concerns, safe travel home and knowing who remains in the building. A strong answer picks at least one and proposes something specific, such as a sign-in and sign-out register with a fixed closing routine, or grouped departures at a set time.\n\nRegister: a council submission is formal, impersonal and organised under headings or clearly signposted paragraphs. First-person appeals ("we really need this") weaken it; so does emotive language. The reader is deciding whether to allocate a public resource and needs to see that the risks have been thought through.\n\nCommon weaknesses: quoting all three figures with no interpretation; arguing at length that studying is important, which nobody disputes; and leaving supervision to the last line, when Source B places it first.',
    referenceAnswerEn: 'This task is self-assessed. What follows is what a strong proposal does, point by point.\n\nUsing the data: a figure must be interpreted, not merely quoted. "68% would use the space" states demand; "41% cite lack of quiet space at home" explains why the demand exists and shows the need is about conditions rather than preference; "22% travel more than 40 minutes" establishes that the existing provision does not reach this group. A proposal that lists all three percentages without saying what each shows has copied rather than used the data.\n\nAnswering Source B: the councillor names supervision as the first obstacle, so the proposal must put supervision early and make it concrete — who supervises, on what rota, at what cost, and who is accountable. A vague promise that "supervision can be arranged" does not meet the constraint the source sets out.\n\nAnswering Source C: the social worker raises two distinct concerns, safe travel home and knowing who remains in the building. A strong answer picks at least one and proposes something specific, such as a sign-in and sign-out register with a fixed closing routine, or grouped departures at a set time.\n\nRegister: a council submission is formal, impersonal and organised under headings or clearly signposted paragraphs. First-person appeals ("we really need this") weaken it; so does emotive language. The reader is deciding whether to allocate a public resource and needs to see that the risks have been thought through.\n\nCommon weaknesses: quoting all three figures with no interpretation; arguing at length that studying is important, which nobody disputes; and leaving supervision to the last line, when Source B places it first.',
    explanation: 'Integrated tasks are marked on whether the sources were used, not on whether they were mentioned. The single most useful habit is to treat each source as setting a question the writing must answer: Source A asks "is there a need, and what kind"; Source B asks "who supervises"; Source C asks "what happens when it ends". A proposal that answers all three reads as considered even if the prose is plain. The register point is worth noting separately: this is a submission to a body that will weigh it against other calls on the same space, so an argument that anticipates the objection is stronger than one that presses the need harder. Blind copying from the data file is the failure this task is designed to expose — a percentage carried across without interpretation adds nothing.',
    explanationEn: 'Integrated tasks are marked on whether the sources were used, not on whether they were mentioned. The single most useful habit is to treat each source as setting a question the writing must answer: Source A asks "is there a need, and what kind"; Source B asks "who supervises"; Source C asks "what happens when it ends". A proposal that answers all three reads as considered even if the prose is plain. The register point is worth noting separately: this is a submission to a body that will weigh it against other calls on the same space, so an argument that anticipates the objection is stronger than one that presses the need harder. Blind copying from the data file is the failure this task is designed to expose — a percentage carried across without interpretation adds nothing.',
    markingScheme: 'Self-assess against four areas.\n\nUse of the data file — at least two figures from Source A, each interpreted rather than quoted; the constraint in Source B answered concretely; at least one concern from Source C addressed with a specific measure.\nTask fulfilment — the piece reads as a proposal to a council, not as an essay; it makes a request the reader could act on.\nOrganisation — a clear structure, one idea per paragraph, the supervision question placed early because Source B places it first.\nLanguage and register — formal, impersonal, accurate; no emotive appeals; vocabulary suited to a public submission.\n\nA fair self-check: cover the data file and reread your proposal. If it still makes sense unchanged, the sources were probably decorative rather than used.',
    markingSchemeEn: 'Self-assess against four areas.\n\nUse of the data file — at least two figures from Source A, each interpreted rather than quoted; the constraint in Source B answered concretely; at least one concern from Source C addressed with a specific measure.\nTask fulfilment — the piece reads as a proposal to a council, not as an essay; it makes a request the reader could act on.\nOrganisation — a clear structure, one idea per paragraph, the supervision question placed early because Source B places it first.\nLanguage and register — formal, impersonal, accurate; no emotive appeals; vocabulary suited to a public submission.\n\nA fair self-check: cover the data file and reread your proposal. If it still makes sense unchanged, the sources were probably decorative rather than used.',
  },
]

// ── 企業、會計與財務概論 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 課程依據：BAFS 必修部分及會計單元。2026 學年起財務報表一律採直式
// （Vertical Form）—— term-guard 攔截 T 字帳／橫式報表字眼。
// 本科不設 LaTeX 數學模式，金額一律以「元」表述，不用貨幣符號。
BATCH.bafs = [
  {
    id: 'bf_w1_01', type: 'text', difficulty: 'basic',
    topicId: 'interest', topicZh: '利息', topicEn: 'Interest',
    question: '甲把 20 000 元存入一個年利率 4%、每年計息一次的定期存款，為期 3 年。乙同樣存入 20 000 元，年利率同為 4%，但以單利計算，同為 3 年。\n試分別計算兩人 3 年後的本利和，並說明兩者差額的來源。',
    questionEn: 'A deposits 20 000 dollars for 3 years at 4% per annum compounded yearly. B deposits 20 000 dollars for 3 years at 4% per annum on a simple-interest basis.\nCalculate the amount each has after 3 years and explain where the difference comes from.',
    referenceAnswer: '甲（複利）：\n本利和 = 20 000 × (1 + 0.04)³ = 20 000 × 1.124864 = 22 497.28 元\n利息 = 2 497.28 元\n\n乙（單利）：\n利息 = 20 000 × 0.04 × 3 = 2 400 元\n本利和 = 22 400 元\n\n差額 = 22 497.28 − 22 400 = 97.28 元。\n差額的來源：複利之下，第一年所得的 800 元利息會在第二年一併計息，第二年所得的利息又會在第三年計息，即「利息本身亦產生利息」。單利則只按原本金 20 000 元計息，三年所得完全相同。\n年期愈長、利率愈高，這個差距擴大得愈快 —— 三年只差 97.28 元，但同一組數字放到二十年，差距會以倍數計。',
    referenceAnswerEn: 'A (compound): 20 000 × 1.04³ = 22 497.28 dollars, interest 2 497.28.\nB (simple): interest = 20 000 × 0.04 × 3 = 2 400, amount 22 400 dollars.\nDifference = 97.28 dollars. Under compounding, the 800 dollars of interest earned in year one itself earns interest in year two, and so on; simple interest is always computed on the original principal only. The gap widens rapidly with time and rate — three years differ by 97.28 dollars, but over twenty years the difference is many times larger.',
    explanation: '本題的計算並不困難，考點在於是否說得出差額的成因。答「因為複利較高」屬複述現象而非解釋機制；正確的說法要指出利息被計入下一期的本金。留意複利公式中的指數是期數而非年數 —— 本題每年計息一次，兩者剛好相同，但若題目改為每半年計息一次，年利率須除以二而期數須乘以二，這是同類題目最常見的失分位置。作答時建議先寫出算式再代入數字，因為評卷對算式本身設有分數。',
    explanationEn: 'The arithmetic is easy; the question is whether the source of the difference can be stated. "Because compound interest is higher" restates the observation instead of explaining the mechanism; the answer must say that interest is added to the principal for the next period. Note that the exponent in the compound formula counts compounding periods, not years — here they coincide, but if interest were compounded half-yearly the rate would be halved and the periods doubled, which is where marks are most often lost. Write the formula before substituting, since the expression itself carries marks.',
  },
  {
    id: 'bf_w1_02', type: 'long', difficulty: 'intermediate', marks: 8, suggestedMinutes: 14,
    topicId: 'depreciation', topicZh: '折舊', topicEn: 'Depreciation',
    question: '某公司於年初以 80 000 元購入一部機器，估計可使用 5 年，5 年後的殘值為 5 000 元。\n（a）以直線法計算每年的折舊額。\n（b）改以餘額遞減法、折舊率 30% 計算首三年的折舊額。\n（c）比較兩種方法在首三年的累計折舊，並說明何種資產較適合採用餘額遞減法。\n（d）有人認為「折舊是公司每年撥出的一筆現金，用以將來重置該機器」。試指出此說法的錯處。',
    questionEn: 'A company buys a machine for 80 000 dollars at the start of the year, with an estimated useful life of 5 years and a residual value of 5 000 dollars.\n(a) Calculate the annual depreciation using the straight-line method.\n(b) Calculate the depreciation for the first three years using the reducing-balance method at 30%.\n(c) Compare the accumulated depreciation over the first three years and state which kind of asset suits the reducing-balance method.\n(d) Someone says depreciation is cash the company sets aside each year to replace the machine. Explain what is wrong with this.',
    referenceAnswer: '（a）每年折舊 = (80 000 − 5 000) ÷ 5 = 15 000 元。\n（b）第一年：80 000 × 30% = 24 000 元，帳面淨值餘 56 000 元\n第二年：56 000 × 30% = 16 800 元，帳面淨值餘 39 200 元\n第三年：39 200 × 30% = 11 760 元，帳面淨值餘 27 440 元\n（c）直線法首三年累計 = 15 000 × 3 = 45 000 元\n餘額遞減法首三年累計 = 24 000 + 16 800 + 11 760 = 52 560 元\n餘額遞減法在前期攤佔較多，故首三年的累計折舊較高。\n適用資產：前期效益高而後期維修成本上升的資產，例如電腦、車輛、生產設備。把折舊費用前重後輕，令折舊與該資產實際帶來的效益在時間上較為配合；後期折舊減少，正好抵銷維修支出上升，令每年的總負擔較為平均。\n（d）折舊並非現金支出，亦沒有任何一筆現金因此而被撥存。折舊是把已付出的成本，按資產可使用的年期分攤到各個會計期間的一項帳面調整，屬於配對原則的應用。公司若要重置機器，必須另行安排資金；帳上有大額累計折舊，不等於銀行戶口有相應的存款。',
    referenceAnswerEn: '(a) (80 000 − 5 000) / 5 = 15 000 dollars per year.\n(b) Year 1: 24 000 (net book value 56 000); Year 2: 16 800 (39 200); Year 3: 11 760 (27 440).\n(c) Straight line accumulates 45 000 over three years; reducing balance accumulates 52 560, so the charge is front-loaded. Reducing balance suits assets whose benefit is greatest early and whose repair costs rise later — computers, vehicles, production equipment — matching the charge to the benefit and offsetting rising repairs with a falling charge.\n(d) Depreciation is not a cash outflow and sets no money aside. It is a book adjustment spreading a cost already incurred across the periods that benefit from the asset, an application of the matching principle. Replacing the machine needs separately arranged funds; a large accumulated depreciation balance does not mean there is money in the bank.',
    explanation: '（d）是本題的重心，亦是 BAFS 最常被誤解的概念之一。折舊之所以令人誤會，是因為它出現在損益表上、令利潤減少，而減少利潤這件事在直覺上與「付了錢」相似。實情是這筆錢在購入資產當日已經付清，折舊只是把它分期記入各年。判斷此類說法的可靠方法是問一句：這項分錄有沒有令銀行結餘改變？答案是沒有，所以它並非現金流。（c）另有一個常見誤解 —— 以為餘額遞減法折舊總額較多。兩種方法的總額同受「成本減殘值」這個上限約束，分別只在時間分佈。',
    explanationEn: 'Part (d) carries the question and targets one of the most misunderstood ideas in the subject. Depreciation misleads because it appears in the income statement and reduces profit, and reducing profit feels like paying money out. In fact the money was paid in full when the asset was bought; depreciation only spreads it across the years. A reliable test for claims of this kind is to ask whether the entry changes the bank balance. It does not, so it is not a cash flow. Part (c) contains a second common error — assuming reducing balance charges more in total. Both methods are bounded by cost less residual value; only the timing differs.',
    markingScheme: '（a）1 分 —— 15 000 元，須先減去殘值。\n（b）3 分 —— 三年各 1 分，每年須以上年的帳面淨值為基數。以原值 80 000 元重複計算者，第二、三年不給分。\n（c）2 分 —— 兩個累計數並作比較（1）；指出適用於前期效益高、後期維修上升的資產並說明配合的理由（1）。\n（d）2 分 —— 指出折舊並非現金支出（1）；說明其為成本分攤的帳面調整、重置須另行籌措資金（1）。',
    markingSchemeEn: '(a) 1 mark — 15 000 dollars, residual value deducted first.\n(b) 3 marks — one per year, each computed on the previous net book value. Repeating 80 000 as the base loses years 2 and 3.\n(c) 2 marks — both accumulated figures compared (1); the suitable asset type with the matching rationale (1).\n(d) 2 marks — depreciation is not a cash outflow (1); it is an allocation of cost, and replacement funds must be arranged separately (1).',
  },
  {
    id: 'bf_w1_03', type: 'long', difficulty: 'intermediate', marks: 8, suggestedMinutes: 14,
    topicId: 'ratios', topicZh: '財務比率', topicEn: 'Financial Ratios',
    question: '某零售公司兩個年度的資料如下（金額以千元計）：\n\n              2025年    2026年\n流動資產        900      1 200\n其中：存貨      400        750\n流動負債        500        600\n\n（a）計算兩年的流動比率及速動比率，答案準確至小數點後兩位。\n（b）該公司經理表示：「流動比率由 1.80 升至 2.00，公司的短期償債能力已見改善。」試評論此說法。\n（c）就（b）所指出的情況，提出兩項可行的改善措施。',
    questionEn: 'A retailer reports the following (in thousands of dollars):\n\n                 2025    2026\nCurrent assets    900   1 200\n  of which stock  400     750\nCurrent liabilities 500    600\n\n(a) Calculate the current ratio and quick ratio for both years, to two decimal places.\n(b) The manager says the current ratio rising from 1.80 to 2.00 shows improved short-term solvency. Comment on this claim.\n(c) Suggest two practical measures to address the situation identified in (b).',
    referenceAnswer: '（a）2025 年：\n流動比率 = 900 ÷ 500 = 1.80\n速動比率 = (900 − 400) ÷ 500 = 500 ÷ 500 = 1.00\n2026 年：\n流動比率 = 1 200 ÷ 600 = 2.00\n速動比率 = (1 200 − 750) ÷ 600 = 450 ÷ 600 = 0.75\n（b）該說法不成立，或至少過於片面。流動比率確實由 1.80 升至 2.00，但速動比率同期由 1.00 跌至 0.75。\n兩者方向相反，原因在於流動資產的增長幾乎全部來自存貨：存貨由 400 增至 750，增幅 350，而流動資產總額只增 300。換言之，非存貨部分的流動資產反而減少了。\n存貨是流動資產之中變現能力最低的一項，須先售出、再收回貨款，才成為現金。以存貨堆砌出來的流動比率上升，並不代表公司有更強的即時償債能力；速動比率跌破 1.00，反而顯示公司已無法單靠速動資產應付到期的流動負債。\n（c）措施一：清理滯銷存貨。以折扣促銷或退回供應商等方式減少存貨水平，把資金釋放為現金或應收帳款，可同時提升速動比率。\n措施二：收緊採購與存貨管理。檢討採購量與補貨週期，避免存貨繼續累積；亦可與供應商商議延長信貸期，改善現金流的時間錯配。',
    referenceAnswerEn: '(a) 2025: current ratio 900/500 = 1.80; quick ratio (900 − 400)/500 = 1.00. 2026: current ratio 1 200/600 = 2.00; quick ratio (1 200 − 750)/600 = 0.75.\n(b) The claim is at best one-sided. The current ratio rose, but the quick ratio fell from 1.00 to 0.75, because almost all the growth in current assets is stock: stock rose by 350 while total current assets rose by only 300, so non-stock current assets actually fell. Stock is the least liquid current asset — it must be sold and the proceeds collected before it becomes cash. A current ratio propped up by stock does not indicate a stronger ability to pay debts as they fall due, and a quick ratio below 1.00 shows the company can no longer cover current liabilities from quick assets alone.\n(c) Clear slow-moving stock through discounting or return to suppliers, converting it into cash or receivables and lifting the quick ratio. Tighten purchasing and stock control — review order quantities and reorder cycles to stop the build-up, and negotiate longer credit terms with suppliers to ease the timing mismatch.',
    explanation: '本題示範比率分析的核心紀律：任何一個比率單獨看都可能誤導，必須與相關比率並讀。流動比率與速動比率的差別只在於是否計入存貨，所以兩者一升一跌，即等於直接指出問題出在存貨。作答時最有力的一步是把兩個增幅並列 —— 存貨增 350 而流動資產總額只增 300，這一句數字比任何形容詞更能說明問題。（c）的措施必須針對（b）所診斷的病因；提出「向銀行借款」之類的建議只會令流動負債上升，反而令兩個比率同時惡化，屬答非所問。',
    explanationEn: 'This shows the central discipline of ratio analysis: a single ratio can mislead and must be read alongside its relatives. The current and quick ratios differ only in whether stock is included, so one rising while the other falls points straight at stock. The strongest single step in an answer is to set the two increases side by side — stock up 350 while total current assets rose only 300 — which says more than any adjective. The measures in (c) must treat the cause diagnosed in (b); suggesting a bank loan would raise current liabilities and worsen both ratios.',
    markingScheme: '（a）4 分 —— 四個比率各 1 分，速動資產須為流動資產減存貨。\n（b）2 分 —— 指出兩個比率方向相反並歸因於存貨增長（1）；說明存貨變現能力最低、速動比率跌破 1.00 的含意（1）。只答「流動比率上升所以改善」全項不給分。\n（c）2 分 —— 兩項措施各 1 分，須針對存貨問題；提出增加借貸等會同時推高流動負債的做法不給分。',
    markingSchemeEn: '(a) 4 marks — one per ratio, with quick assets taken as current assets less stock.\n(b) 2 marks — the two ratios move in opposite directions, attributed to stock (1); the illiquidity of stock and the meaning of a quick ratio below 1.00 (1). Accepting the manager’s claim earns nothing.\n(c) 2 marks — one per measure, each addressing stock. Proposals such as further borrowing, which raise current liabilities, earn nothing.',
  },
  {
    id: 'bf_w1_04', type: 'text', difficulty: 'intermediate',
    topicId: 'costing', topicZh: '成本與定價', topicEn: 'Costing & Pricing',
    question: '某小店售賣單一產品，每件售價 60 元，每件變動成本 36 元，每月固定成本 21 600 元。\n（a）計算每月的收支平衡銷量。\n（b）若該店希望每月賺取 6 000 元利潤，須售出多少件？\n（c）若租金上升令固定成本增至 24 000 元，而售價與變動成本不變，收支平衡銷量會如何改變？',
    questionEn: 'A shop sells one product at 60 dollars each, with a variable cost of 36 dollars per unit and fixed costs of 21 600 dollars per month.\n(a) Find the monthly break-even quantity.\n(b) How many units must be sold to earn a monthly profit of 6 000 dollars?\n(c) If rent raises fixed costs to 24 000 dollars while price and variable cost are unchanged, how does the break-even quantity change?',
    referenceAnswer: '每件貢獻邊際 = 60 − 36 = 24 元。\n（a）收支平衡銷量 = 固定成本 ÷ 每件貢獻邊際 = 21 600 ÷ 24 = 900 件。\n（b）所需銷量 = (固定成本 + 目標利潤) ÷ 每件貢獻邊際 = (21 600 + 6 000) ÷ 24 = 27 600 ÷ 24 = 1 150 件。\n（c）新的收支平衡銷量 = 24 000 ÷ 24 = 1 000 件，較原來增加 100 件。\n固定成本上升 2 400 元，而每件貢獻邊際不變，故須多售 2 400 ÷ 24 = 100 件才能覆蓋新增的固定成本。',
    referenceAnswerEn: 'Contribution per unit = 60 − 36 = 24 dollars.\n(a) 21 600 / 24 = 900 units.\n(b) (21 600 + 6 000) / 24 = 1 150 units.\n(c) 24 000 / 24 = 1 000 units, an increase of 100. Fixed costs rose by 2 400 while contribution per unit is unchanged, so 2 400 / 24 = 100 extra units are needed.',
    explanation: '本題三部分共用同一條公式，變化只在分子。關鍵在於分母永遠是「每件貢獻邊際」，即售價減變動成本，而非售價本身 —— 用 60 元作分母是最常見的失分，得出的數字看似合理卻偏小。（c）除了直接重算之外，亦可用增量思維：固定成本增加多少，除以每件貢獻邊際，即得須多售的件數。能寫出這一步，顯示理解公式的結構而非僅記住形式。留意目標利潤在公式中與固定成本同列於分子，因為兩者同樣須由貢獻邊際去覆蓋。',
    explanationEn: 'All three parts use one formula with different numerators. The denominator is always the contribution per unit, price less variable cost, not the price itself — using 60 is the commonest error and yields a plausible-looking but too-small figure. Part (c) can be answered incrementally as well as by recomputation: divide the rise in fixed costs by the contribution per unit. Showing that step demonstrates understanding of the structure rather than recall of the form. Note that the target profit sits in the numerator alongside fixed costs because both must be covered by contribution.',
  },
  {
    id: 'bf_w1_05', type: 'long', difficulty: 'hard', marks: 10, suggestedMinutes: 18,
    topicId: 'financial_statements', topicZh: '財務報表', topicEn: 'Financial Statements',
    question: '某獨資商店於 2026 年 3 月 31 日的資料如下（金額以元計）：\n\n銷貨 480 000；銷貨成本 312 000；租金 48 000；薪金 66 000；水電 9 600；折舊 12 000；利息支出 4 800\n\n（a）以直式（Vertical Form）編製截至 2026 年 3 月 31 日止年度的損益表，須列出毛利及本年度純利。\n（b）計算毛利率及純利率，答案準確至小數點後一位。\n（c）該店東主表示：「純利率偏低，應該立即減少薪金支出。」試從兩個角度評論此建議。',
    questionEn: 'A sole trader reports the following at 31 March 2026 (in dollars):\n\nSales 480 000; cost of goods sold 312 000; rent 48 000; salaries 66 000; utilities 9 600; depreciation 12 000; interest expense 4 800.\n\n(a) Prepare the income statement for the year ended 31 March 2026 in vertical form, showing gross profit and profit for the year.\n(b) Calculate the gross profit margin and the net profit margin, to one decimal place.\n(c) The owner says the low net margin means salaries should be cut immediately. Comment on this from two angles.',
    referenceAnswer: '（a）損益表（直式），截至 2026 年 3 月 31 日止年度：\n\n銷貨                        480 000\n減：銷貨成本                312 000\n毛利                        168 000\n\n減：營業開支\n  租金            48 000\n  薪金            66 000\n  水電             9 600\n  折舊            12 000\n                            135 600\n營業溢利                     32 400\n減：利息支出                  4 800\n本年度純利                   27 600\n\n（b）毛利率 = 168 000 ÷ 480 000 × 100% = 35.0%\n純利率 = 27 600 ÷ 480 000 × 100% = 5.8%\n（c）角度一 —— 該建議未經診斷即開藥。毛利率 35.0% 與純利率 5.8% 之間相差 29.2 個百分點，全部由營業開支及利息造成，而薪金 66 000 元只佔其中一部分；租金 48 000 元的比重同樣可觀。在未比較同業水平、未檢視各項開支是否合理之前，單獨挑出薪金並無依據。\n角度二 —— 減薪可能損害收入本身。零售業務依賴人手提供服務，減少薪金支出通常意味減少人手或降低待遇，可能令服務水準下降、員工流失，銷貨隨之減少。若銷貨下跌的幅度大於節省的薪金，純利反而更低。\n可行的替代方向包括：檢視租金是否可重議、提高毛利率（改善採購成本或調整售價組合）、以及檢討利息支出所反映的負債水平。',
    referenceAnswerEn: '(a) Income statement (vertical form) for the year ended 31 March 2026:\nSales 480 000; less cost of goods sold 312 000; gross profit 168 000.\nLess operating expenses: rent 48 000, salaries 66 000, utilities 9 600, depreciation 12 000, total 135 600; operating profit 32 400.\nLess interest expense 4 800; profit for the year 27 600.\n(b) Gross margin = 168 000 / 480 000 = 35.0%; net margin = 27 600 / 480 000 = 5.8%.\n(c) First, the proposal prescribes before diagnosing. The 29.2 percentage points between the two margins are consumed by operating expenses and interest, of which salaries of 66 000 are only part; rent at 48 000 is comparable. Singling out salaries without comparing industry norms or reviewing each expense has no basis.\nSecond, cutting pay may damage revenue itself. Retail depends on staff to deliver service; lower salary spending usually means fewer or less well-paid staff, risking poorer service and turnover, and hence lower sales. If sales fall by more than the saving, profit is worse.\nAlternatives include renegotiating rent, improving the gross margin through purchasing or pricing, and reviewing the borrowing implied by the interest charge.',
    explanation: '（a）的格式本身設有分數：直式報表要求由銷貨開始，逐級減至純利，中間必須明確列出毛利與營業溢利兩個小計。把利息支出與租金、薪金並列於營業開支之內是常見的格式錯誤 —— 利息屬融資成本，須在營業溢利之後單獨扣減，否則營業溢利這一個小計便失去意義。（c）示範一種在近年卷二經常出現的問法：東主的建議並非全錯，但推理跳步。良好的答案先指出診斷不足，再指出該措施本身可能反噬，最後提出替代方向；只答「不應減薪，因為員工會不開心」屬情緒判斷，未能取得分數。',
    explanationEn: 'The format in (a) carries marks in itself: a vertical statement runs from sales down to profit, with gross profit and operating profit shown as explicit subtotals. Listing interest alongside rent and salaries within operating expenses is the standard formatting error — interest is a financing cost deducted after operating profit, otherwise that subtotal loses its meaning. Part (c) illustrates a question type common in recent papers: the owner is not simply wrong but reasons in one leap. A good answer notes the missing diagnosis, then the risk that the measure backfires, then offers alternatives. "Do not cut pay because staff will be unhappy" is an emotional judgement and earns nothing.',
    markingScheme: '（a）5 分 —— 毛利 168 000（1）；四項營業開支齊全並加總為 135 600（2）；營業溢利 32 400 作為獨立小計（1）；利息在營業溢利之後扣減，純利 27 600（1）。把利息計入營業開支者扣 1 分。\n（b）2 分 —— 毛利率 35.0%（1）；純利率 5.8%（1）。\n（c）3 分 —— 指出未經比較即單挑薪金、其他開支比重同樣可觀（1）；指出減薪可能令銷貨下跌而反致純利更低（1）；提出至少一項可行的替代方向（1）。',
    markingSchemeEn: '(a) 5 marks — gross profit 168 000 (1); all four operating expenses totalling 135 600 (2); operating profit 32 400 as a separate subtotal (1); interest deducted after it, profit for the year 27 600 (1). Treating interest as an operating expense loses 1 mark.\n(b) 2 marks — 35.0% (1); 5.8% (1).\n(c) 3 marks — salaries singled out without comparison while other expenses are comparable (1); the risk that lower pay reduces sales and hence profit (1); at least one workable alternative (1).',
  },
]

